#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hapax-obsidian-sync-preflight — validate an Obsidian Sync exclusion list and
predict the upload set BEFORE a seed sync runs.

Why this exists (measured, 2026-09-14, task obsidian-sync-freshvault-headless-20260914):
``ob sync-config --excluded-folders`` accepts any string. The client matches each
entry as a literal prefix of the path it EMITS — in ``obsidian-headless`` 0.0.14,
``_allowSyncFile(e, t)`` begins::

    for (let r of this.ignoreFolders)
        if (t && e === r || e.startsWith(r + "/")) return !1;

so an entry that is not a real prefix in that emitted namespace matches nothing,
reports no error, and the data uploads. The failure mode is **silent admission**,
and the only witness is a size readback taken after the upload already happened.

Three separate hand-transcriptions of one 14/15/17-entry exclusion list each
introduced at least one unmatchable path: ``terrain1-chunks-lex`` and
``terrain1-chunks-sem`` (neither directory exists), then
``ingestion-gold/ocr/pages`` missing its ``30-areas/hapax/`` prefix — the last
would have leaked 196 PNGs / 379.5 MiB past an exclusion the operator had
explicitly accepted. A path list carried by hand is a transcription channel with
no checksum; this is the checksum.

The single predicate is: **does this entry name a directory in the namespace ob
will compare against?** That subsumes every failure shape — a missing path, a
wrong prefix, a file instead of a folder, a trailing slash (ob compares raw, so
``x/`` probes ``x//``), a ``.``/``..`` component the filesystem resolves but ob
never emits, and a Unicode form that exists on disk but not after ob's nonbreaking
space rewrite and NFC normalization. Filesystem paths are kept separately from
emitted paths precisely because those two namespaces are not the same.

It also predicts the upload set by replaying the client's admission rules, so a
seed has an expected byte/file count to verify against rather than one derived by
subtracting estimates.

Usage:
    hapax-obsidian-sync-preflight VAULT --excluded-folders a,b,c [--file-types ...]
    hapax-obsidian-sync-preflight VAULT --from-sync-config          # audit the LIVE list
    hapax-obsidian-sync-preflight VAULT --from-sync-config --json

Exit: 0 list valid (prediction printed); 2 refused — an entry cannot match, so the
list would fail open; 3 usage error, IO error, no sync config, or an incomplete
traversal (a partial total must never read as successful verification).

Recheck every claim in this docstring — the exit statuses and each replayed rule:

    uv run pytest tests/scripts/test_hapax_obsidian_sync_preflight.py -q

Recheck the rules against the CLIENT (they are read off a minified bundle, so they
can drift with any upgrade; `npm ls -g obsidian-headless` gives the pinned version):

    node -e 'const s=require("fs").readFileSync(process.env.OB_CLI,"utf8");
      for (const k of ["function Ne(","function Cs(","function Is(",
      "_allowSyncFile(","reconcileSymbolicLinkCreation(","Xc=","me=["])
      console.log(k, s.slice(s.indexOf(k), s.indexOf(k)+160), "\n")'

Rules replayed from obsidian-headless 0.0.14 ``cli.js``:
  * emitted namespace — ``Ne(s) = Cs(_e(s)).normalize("NFC")``, where ``Cs``
    rewrites U+00A0 and U+202F to ordinary spaces. ``ignoreFolders`` entries are
    then compared to that literally, so both the space rewrite and NFC matter.
  * entry trimming — ob trims each comma-separated CLI field with JavaScript
    ``trim()`` semantics, which are NOT Python's ``str.strip()``. A PERSISTED list
    is not trimmed at all; it is compared byte for byte.
  * hidden pruning — ``Is(s)`` walks every path component and rejects the path if
    ANY component starts with ``.``; the adapter treats such paths as absent.
  * symlinks — ``reconcileSymbolicLinkCreation`` resolves the link and RETURNS when
    the target overlaps an already-watched resolved path. The vault root is always
    watched, so a link INTO the vault (or to a directory containing it) is skipped
    entirely, while a link escaping to a disjoint subtree is followed. Only a
    DIRECTORY link then becomes watched (the watcher is installed in the
    ``isDirectory`` branch), so a file link is admitted without reserving its target.
    The overlap rule is also what bounds the walk.
  * the config scan is NOT a walk — it enumerates fixed shapes through
    ``adapter.list``/``exists``/``stat``, which follow symlinks and never consult the
    watcher set, so the overlap rule does not apply there and no deeper nesting is
    reached. That queue only builds the local file index; admission still runs through
    ``_allowSyncFile``, which is why the literal ``<cfg>/config`` file is enumerated
    but never uploaded. ``ws``/``Ss`` also require ``configDir`` to be a dotfolder name
    with no separators, and throw otherwise.
  * per-file size — ``!m.folder && m.size > perFileMax`` is skipped with
    ``File too large to sync``. The client's fallback is 199 MiB, but the SERVER may
    send its own at login, which is not observable locally — hence ``--per-file-max``.
  * ``--file-types`` is parsed by ``Kr``: split on ``,``, trim, lowercase, and throw
    for any field not in ``us`` — an EMPTY field included, so ``,`` and ``image,`` are
    rejected. Only the exact empty string restores the defaults.

Where the client's own outcome is not determinate, this refuses instead of guessing:
``listRecursive`` scans siblings concurrently, so two directory links resolving to one
target can both clear the overlap check before either installs its watcher, and
whether one or both upload depends on scheduling.
  * exclusions precede everything — the ``ignoreFolders`` loop runs before the
    hidden-path check and before the config-dir branch, so an entry naming a hidden
    or config-dir path does match.
  * attachment classes — ``md``/``canvas``/``base`` always sync; the rest gate on
    the selection, and anything in no class is ``unsupported`` (off by default).
    ``webm`` counts as audio OR video.
  * the empty selection — ``--file-types ""`` DELETES the stored key and so
    restores the defaults, whereas a persisted ``allowTypes: []`` is honoured as
    "no attachments" (``[] || me`` keeps ``[]``: an empty array is truthy in JS).
  * config-dir uploads — when ``allowSpecialFiles`` is non-empty ob also uploads
    matching files from the config dir (default ``.obsidian``) through a separate
    hidden walk, so they are counted even though the main walk prunes dotted paths.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import sys
import unicodedata
from pathlib import Path

OK = 0
REFUSED = 2
ERROR = 3

# obsidian-headless 0.0.14 cli.js: Vt, Wt, Ht, Gt, fo, ks, ho
IMAGE = frozenset({"bmp", "png", "jpg", "jpeg", "gif", "svg", "webp", "avif"})
AUDIO = frozenset({"mp3", "wav", "m4a", "3gp", "flac", "ogg", "oga", "opus"})
VIDEO = frozenset({"mp4", "webm", "ogv", "mov", "mkv"})
PDF = frozenset({"pdf"})
ALWAYS = frozenset({"md", "canvas", "base"})  # native: never gated on the selection
VALID_FILE_TYPES = ("image", "audio", "video", "pdf", "unsupported")
DEFAULT_FILE_TYPES = ("image", "audio", "pdf", "video")  # cli.js `me`

# The PERSISTED config.json and the `sync-status --json` OUTPUT use different key
# names for the same settings. cli.js writes the live object (`ys(t.vaultId, t)`)
# with `ignoreFolders` / `allowTypes` / `allowSpecialFiles`, and only the printer
# `_r(t)` renames them: `excludedFolders: s.ignoreFolders||[]`,
# `fileTypes: s.allowTypes||me`, `configs: s.allowSpecialFiles||[]`. Reading the
# PRINTED names out of the FILE finds nothing — measured against a real config
# holding 17 exclusions, which is why this mapping is pinned by tests.
PERSISTED_EXCLUSIONS_KEY = "ignoreFolders"
PERSISTED_FILE_TYPES_KEY = "allowTypes"
# A config written by ob always carries these; used to tell "no exclusions
# configured" apart from "this file is not an ob sync config / schema moved".
PERSISTED_REQUIRED_KEYS = ("vaultId", "vaultPath", "conflictStrategy")
# Populated below once the category list exists. Maps every persisted list field the
# tool READS to its allowed values (None = any strings). Validation is driven by this
# table so a newly-read field cannot silently bypass the shape check.
PERSISTED_LIST_FIELDS: dict[str, tuple[str, ...] | None] = {}


class _Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    """argparse exits 2 on a usage error, which would collide with REFUSED — and
    REFUSED is the load-bearing status for scripting. Usage errors exit 3."""

    def error(self, message: str) -> None:  # type: ignore[override]
        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)
        print(
            f"{self.prog}: error: {message}. Next: pass the vault path plus exactly "
            "one of --excluded-folders or --from-sync-config; see --help.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        raise SystemExit(ERROR)


# cli.js: `Ne(s) = Cs(_e(s)).normalize("NFC")` where `Cs(s) = s.replace(Xc," ")` and
# `Xc = / | /g` — so the emitted name has nonbreaking (U+00A0) and narrow
# nonbreaking (U+202F) spaces rewritten to ORDINARY spaces before NFC runs. Held as
# codepoints rather than literals because the characters are invisible in source.
# `_e` only collapses repeated slashes and trims leading/trailing ones, a no-op for
# the relative paths a directory walk builds, so it is deliberately not replicated:
# doing so would mask a malformed path we construct ourselves.
_EMITTED_SPACE_CODEPOINTS = (0x00A0, 0x202F)


def _emitted(rel: str) -> str:
    """The path ob compares exclusions against — cli.js ``Ne(s)``."""
    for codepoint in _EMITTED_SPACE_CODEPOINTS:
        rel = rel.replace(chr(codepoint), " ")
    return unicodedata.normalize("NFC", rel)


# ECMAScript WhiteSpace + LineTerminator, i.e. exactly what String.prototype.trim
# removes. Python's str.strip() is NOT the same set — measured: it also strips
# U+001C-U+001F and U+0085 (which JS keeps), and it does NOT strip U+FEFF (which JS
# removes). Using str.strip() certified entries that ob then failed to match.
_JS_TRIM_CHARS = "".join(
    (
        "\t\n\v\f\r ",
        "      　﻿",
        *(chr(c) for c in range(0x2000, 0x200B)),
    )
)


def _js_trim(value: str) -> str:
    """Reproduce cli.js's ``.map(n => n.trim())`` on comma-split CLI input."""
    return value.strip(_JS_TRIM_CHARS)


def _extension(name: str) -> str:
    """Mirror cli.js ``$(s)``: last dot wins, lowercased, no leading-dot match."""
    idx = name.rfind(".")
    if idx in (-1, 0, len(name) - 1):
        return ""
    return name[idx + 1 :].lower()


def _classify(ext: str) -> str:
    if ext in ALWAYS:
        return "native"
    if ext in IMAGE:
        return "image"
    if ext == "webm":
        return "audio_or_video"
    if ext in AUDIO:
        return "audio"
    if ext in VIDEO:
        return "video"
    if ext in PDF:
        return "pdf"
    return "unsupported"


def _admitted(klass: str, file_types: frozenset[str]) -> bool:
    if klass == "native":
        return True
    if klass == "audio_or_video":
        return bool({"audio", "video"} & file_types)
    return klass in file_types


def resolve_entry(vault: Path, entry: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Can ``entry`` match anything ob emits? Returns (effective, detail).

    Descends component by component, comparing each against the NFC form of the
    real directory names, because that is the only namespace ob's literal prefix
    test ever sees. Cheap: one listdir per component, never a full walk.
    """
    if not entry:
        return False, "empty entry"
    if entry.startswith("/") or Path(entry).is_absolute():
        return False, "not vault-relative; ob emits relative paths only"
    if entry.endswith("/") or "//" in entry:
        return (
            False,
            "trailing or doubled slash — ob compares the raw string, so this "
            "probes a path with an empty component and can never match",
        )
    parts = entry.split("/")
    if any(part in (".", "..") for part in parts):
        return (
            False,
            "contains a '.' or '..' component; the filesystem resolves it but ob "
            "emits normalized relative paths that never contain dot segments",
        )
    # NOTE: no refusal for a dotted component. ob runs the ignoreFolders loop BEFORE
    # the hidden-path check, so a hidden entry does match — and it is load-bearing for
    # the config dir, whose files DO sync when `configs` is enabled. Refusing these
    # rejected valid config-dir exclusions. Ones that cannot matter are annotated as
    # redundant by the caller instead.

    current = vault
    for depth, part in enumerate(parts):
        try:
            children = os.listdir(current)
        except OSError as exc:
            return False, f"cannot read {'/'.join(parts[:depth]) or '.'}: {exc.strerror}"
        # ob would compare against the NFC form of the on-disk name.
        match = next((c for c in children if _emitted(c) == part), None)
        if match is None:
            literal = next((c for c in children if c == part), None)
            if literal is not None:
                return (
                    False,
                    f"component {part!r} exists on disk but not in ob's emitted "
                    "namespace: ob rewrites nonbreaking spaces to ordinary ones and "
                    "NFC-normalizes the path, then matches exclusions literally, so "
                    f"this entry can never match. Use {_emitted(literal)!r}.",
                )
            return False, f"no such path in the vault (component {part!r} not found)"
        current = current / match
        if not os.path.isdir(current):
            return (
                False,
                "names a file; ob's exclusion is a folder-prefix test, so this "
                "entry excludes nothing",
            )
    return True, ""


def validate_entries(
    vault: Path,
    entries: list[str],
    *,
    trim: bool,
    config_dir: str = ".obsidian",
    configs_enabled: bool = False,
) -> list[dict]:
    """Classify each exclusion entry. Anything not ``ok`` cannot match and so
    fails OPEN — the data it names would upload.

    ``trim`` reproduces what the client does to the input it is given, and nothing
    more. ob trims each comma-separated CLI field, so a CLI list is trimmed with
    JS semantics; but a PERSISTED list is compared byte for byte, so trimming it
    here would certify an entry like ``"private "`` that ob can never match.
    """
    findings: list[dict] = []
    for raw in entries:
        entry = _js_trim(raw) if trim else raw
        finding: dict = {"entry": raw, "normalized": entry}
        effective, detail = resolve_entry(vault, entry)
        if effective:
            finding["verdict"] = "ok"
        else:
            finding.update(verdict="unmatchable", detail=detail)
        findings.append(finding)

    # Redundancy is not a refusal, but it hides intent — report it.
    ok_entries = [f["normalized"] for f in findings if f["verdict"] == "ok"]
    for finding in findings:
        if finding["verdict"] != "ok":
            continue
        entry = finding["normalized"]
        covered_by = [
            other for other in ok_entries if other != entry and entry.startswith(other + "/")
        ]
        if covered_by:
            finding["redundant_under"] = covered_by
        parts = entry.split("/")
        # ob's Is() rejects a path when ANY component is dotted, so a dotted
        # component at any depth means nothing under this entry was going to sync —
        # unless it is the config dir and config syncing is on, which is the one
        # hidden subtree ob does reach.
        if any(p.startswith(".") for p in parts) and not (
            parts[0] == config_dir and configs_enabled
        ):
            finding["no_effect_reason"] = (
                "config syncing is disabled, so nothing under the config dir syncs"
                if parts[0] == config_dir
                else "hidden path: nothing under it syncs anyway"
            )
    return findings


def _excluded(rel: str, entries: list[str]) -> bool:
    return any(rel == e or rel.startswith(e + "/") for e in entries)


PERSISTED_CONFIGS_KEY = "allowSpecialFiles"
_PLUGIN_FILES = frozenset({"manifest.json", "main.js", "styles.css", "data.json"})
# cli.js `us` / `hs`: the categories `configs` may name.
VALID_CONFIG_CATEGORIES = (
    "app",
    "appearance",
    "appearance-data",
    "hotkey",
    "core-plugin",
    "core-plugin-data",
    "community-plugin",
    "community-plugin-data",
)
PERSISTED_LIST_FIELDS.update(
    {
        PERSISTED_EXCLUSIONS_KEY: None,  # any string is a candidate path
        PERSISTED_FILE_TYPES_KEY: VALID_FILE_TYPES,
        PERSISTED_CONFIGS_KEY: VALID_CONFIG_CATEGORIES,
    }
)


def _config_category(rel_in_config_dir: str) -> str | None:
    """Which ``configs`` category a file under the config dir belongs to.

    Mirrors the ``e.startsWith(configDir + "/")`` branch of cli.js
    ``_allowSyncFile``. Returns None when the file is never synced.
    """
    parts = rel_in_config_dir.split("/")
    if any(p == "node_modules" or p.startswith(".") for p in parts):
        return None
    if rel_in_config_dir in ("workspace.json", "workspace-mobile.json"):
        return None
    base = parts[-1]
    ext = _extension(base)
    if rel_in_config_dir in ("app.json", "types.json"):
        return "app"
    if rel_in_config_dir == "appearance.json":
        return "appearance"
    if rel_in_config_dir == "hotkeys.json":
        return "hotkey"
    if rel_in_config_dir in ("core-plugins.json", "core-plugins-migration.json"):
        return "core-plugin"
    if rel_in_config_dir == "community-plugins.json":
        return "community-plugin"
    if (parts[0] == "themes" and len(parts) == 3 and base in ("theme.css", "manifest.json")) or (
        parts[0] == "snippets" and len(parts) == 2 and ext == "css"
    ):
        return "appearance-data"
    if len(parts) == 1 and ext == "json":
        return "core-plugin-data"
    if parts[0] == "plugins" and len(parts) == 3 and base in _PLUGIN_FILES:
        return "community-plugin-data"
    return None


def predict_config_uploads(
    vault: Path,
    config_dir: str,
    configs: frozenset[str],
    entries: list[str],
) -> dict:
    """The config-dir files ob uploads when ``configs`` is non-empty.

    Without this, auditing a vault that HAS config syncing enabled under-predicts by
    the whole config directory — the main walk prunes every dotted path, and the
    config dir is dotted by default. ob reaches it through its own scan, which is a
    bounded enumeration rather than a walk; see the comment below.
    """
    root = vault / config_dir
    total_bytes = files = 0
    by_category: dict[str, list[int]] = {}
    # Errors flow to the SAME exit-3 guard the main walk uses; a partial total here
    # must not read as successful verification either.
    errors: list[dict] = []

    def _on_error(exc: OSError) -> None:
        errors.append({"path": str(exc.filename), "error": exc.strerror})

    def _result() -> dict:
        return {
            "bytes": total_bytes,
            "files": files,
            "by_category": {
                k: {"bytes": v[0], "files": v[1]} for k, v in sorted(by_category.items())
            },
            "config_dir": config_dir,
            "traversal_errors": errors,
        }

    if not configs or not root.is_dir():
        return _result()

    def _listdir(path: Path) -> list[str]:
        try:
            return sorted(os.listdir(path))
        except OSError as exc:
            _on_error(exc)
            return []

    # The config scan is NOT a recursive walk. cli.js enumerates a fixed set of
    # shapes through `adapter.list` / `exists` / `stat` (all of which FOLLOW symlinks,
    # and none of which consult the watcher set, so the link-overlap rule does not
    # apply here): the literal file `<cfg>/config`, top-level `*.json`,
    # `themes/<dir>/{manifest.json,theme.css}`, `snippets/*.css`, and
    # `plugins/<dir>/<isPluginFile>`. Modelling it as a recursive walk both invented
    # depths ob never reaches and, via the shared link policy, suppressed aliases ob
    # does upload.
    candidates: list[str] = ["config"]
    candidates += [n for n in _listdir(root) if _extension(n) == "json"]
    themes = root / "themes"
    if themes.is_dir():
        for theme in _listdir(themes):
            candidates += [
                f"themes/{theme}/manifest.json",
                f"themes/{theme}/theme.css",
            ]
    snippets = root / "snippets"
    if snippets.is_dir():
        candidates += [f"snippets/{n}" for n in _listdir(snippets) if _extension(n) == "css"]
    plugins = root / "plugins"
    if plugins.is_dir():
        for plugin in _listdir(plugins):
            candidates += [f"plugins/{plugin}/{n}" for n in sorted(_PLUGIN_FILES)]

    for rel in candidates:
        rel_emitted = _emitted(rel)
        # ob runs the ignoreFolders loop BEFORE the config-dir branch, so an
        # exclusion naming a config path excludes it.
        if _excluded(f"{_emitted(config_dir)}/{rel_emitted}", entries):
            continue
        category = _config_category(rel_emitted)
        if category is None or category not in configs:
            continue
        candidate = root / rel
        try:
            stat = os.stat(candidate)  # follows symlinks, as adapter.stat does
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue  # `exists()` is false — ob drops it, not an error
        except OSError as exc:
            _on_error(exc)
            continue
        if not os.path.isfile(candidate):
            continue  # ob requires stat().type === "file"
        total_bytes += stat.st_size
        files += 1
        bucket = by_category.setdefault(category, [0, 0])
        bucket[0] += stat.st_size
        bucket[1] += 1
    return _result()


def _overlaps(a: str, b: str) -> bool:
    """Whether two resolved paths are the same subtree, either way round."""
    return a == b or a.startswith(b + os.sep) or b.startswith(a + os.sep)


# cli.js `Zi`: `this.perFileMax = 199*1024*1024`, replaced by the server's
# `perFileMax` in the login handshake when it sends one. The seed loop then does
# `!m.folder && m.size > e.perFileMax` -> `logSkip("File too large to sync")`, so a
# file STRICTLY larger than the limit never uploads. This is why the audit's "33 files
# >= 200 MB were never synced" was true without anyone configuring it.
DEFAULT_PER_FILE_MAX = 199 * 1024 * 1024


class _LinkPolicy:
    """ob's symlink rule, in ONE place for every walk and every entry kind.

    ``reconcileSymbolicLinkCreation`` resolves the link and RETURNS when the target
    overlaps the resolved path of an already-watched path — before it distinguishes a
    file from a directory. The vault root is always watched, so a link into the vault
    (or to a directory containing it) is reconciled through neither route; a link
    escaping to a disjoint subtree is followed and becomes watched itself.

    This was patched three times as separate cases — directory links, then file links,
    then the config dir — which is the signal to stop patching and state the rule
    once. Registration happens only for links the caller has already decided are
    visible: a hidden link is rejected by ob before its target is ever registered, so
    registering it here would let a hidden link suppress a visible upload of the same
    target, and make the result depend on traversal order.
    """

    def __init__(self, vault: Path) -> None:
        # The root watcher is installed by `watch()` BEFORE `listAll()` runs, so
        # overlap with it is deterministic. Overlap with another LINK's target is not:
        # `listRecursive` pushes every child's scan and awaits Promise.all, so two
        # aliases can both clear the overlap check before either installs its watcher.
        self.root_targets = [os.path.realpath(vault)]
        self.link_targets: list[str] = []
        self.skipped: list[dict] = []
        self.escaping: list[dict] = []
        self.ambiguous: list[dict] = []

    def follow(self, abspath: str, relpath: str, on_error) -> bool:
        try:
            target = os.path.realpath(abspath)
        except OSError as exc:  # pragma: no cover
            on_error(exc)
            return False
        if any(_overlaps(target, seen) for seen in self.root_targets):
            self.skipped.append({"path": relpath, "target": target})
            return False
        racing = [t for t in self.link_targets if _overlaps(target, t)]
        if racing:
            # Whether this alias uploads depends on which concurrent scan installs its
            # watcher first. There is no exact answer to report.
            self.ambiguous.append({"path": relpath, "target": target})
            return False
        # Only a DIRECTORY link becomes watched: cli.js installs the watcher in the
        # `a.isDirectory()` branch (`Yi && startWatchPath(t)`), while the isFile branch
        # goes straight to reconcileFileCreation. So a file link is admitted without
        # reserving its target — reserving it would make one file link suppress a
        # later, legitimate upload of the same or an overlapping path.
        if os.path.isdir(target):
            self.link_targets.append(target)
        self.escaping.append({"path": relpath, "target": target})
        return True


def predict(
    vault: Path,
    entries: list[str],
    file_types: frozenset[str],
    links: _LinkPolicy,
    per_file_max: int,
) -> dict:
    by_class: dict[str, list[int]] = {}
    by_ext: dict[str, list[int]] = {}
    by_top: dict[str, list[int]] = {}
    upload_bytes = upload_files = 0
    largest: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    traversal_errors: list[dict] = []
    too_large: list[dict] = []

    def _on_error(exc: OSError) -> None:
        # A skipped subtree makes every total below a floor, not a measurement.
        traversal_errors.append({"path": str(exc.filename), "error": exc.strerror})

    # ob's symlink rule, read off cli.js `reconcileSymbolicLinkCreation`: it resolves
    # the link, and if the target OVERLAPS the resolved path of an already-watched
    # path it RETURNS — reconciling nothing. The vault root is always watched
    # (`startWatchPath("/")`), so a link whose target is inside the vault, or which
    # contains the vault, is skipped entirely; only a link that escapes to a disjoint
    # subtree is descended into, and it then becomes watched itself, so a later link
    # overlapping IT is skipped too.
    #
    # Measured both halves: the live vault's `20-projects/hapax-research` escapes to
    # a source repo and the seed uploaded 678 markdown files / 11.8 MiB from behind
    # it, so escaping links must be followed. An earlier revision of this walk went
    # further and counted a vault-INTERNAL alias under both routes; that over-predicts,
    # because ob skips it. The overlap rule is also the cycle guard: any link back
    # into the vault, or to an ancestor of it, overlaps and is pruned.
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(vault, followlinks=True, onerror=_on_error):
        rel_dir = os.path.relpath(dirpath, vault)
        rel_dir = "" if rel_dir == "." else _emitted(rel_dir)

        def _rel(name: str, _rel_dir: str = rel_dir) -> str:
            return f"{_rel_dir}/{_emitted(name)}" if _rel_dir else _emitted(name)

        # Prune FIRST. Pruning every dotted directory at every level is equivalent to
        # cli.js ``Is(s)`` ("hidden if ANY path component is dotted"), because no
        # descendant of a pruned directory is ever visited; dotted FILES are skipped
        # by name below. Pruning must precede link registration: ob rejects a hidden
        # path before registering its target, so registering a hidden link's target
        # here would let it suppress a visible link to the same place.
        dirnames[:] = [
            d for d in dirnames if not d.startswith(".") and not _excluded(_rel(d), entries)
        ]
        # Then apply ob's link rule to what survives.
        for name in list(dirnames):
            child = os.path.join(dirpath, name)
            if os.path.islink(child) and not links.follow(child, _rel(name), _on_error):
                dirnames.remove(name)

        for name in filenames:
            if name.startswith("."):
                continue
            rel = f"{rel_dir}/{_emitted(name)}" if rel_dir else _emitted(name)
            if _excluded(rel, entries):
                continue
            child = os.path.join(dirpath, name)
            # The overlap rule applies to FILE links too: ob rejects on overlap before
            # it distinguishes a file from a directory, so an in-vault file alias is
            # not uploaded even though os.stat would happily follow it.
            if os.path.islink(child) and not links.follow(child, rel, _on_error):
                continue
            try:
                size = os.stat(child).st_size
            except OSError as exc:
                _on_error(exc)
                continue
            klass = _classify(_extension(name))
            bucket = by_class.setdefault(klass, [0, 0])
            bucket[0] += size
            bucket[1] += 1
            if not _admitted(klass, file_types):
                continue
            if size > per_file_max:
                # cli.js logSkip("File too large to sync"): admitted by class, then
                # dropped by size. Counting it would overstate the upload.
                too_large.append({"path": rel, "bytes": size})
                continue
            upload_bytes += size
            upload_files += 1
            ext = by_ext.setdefault(_extension(name), [0, 0])
            ext[0] += size
            ext[1] += 1
            top = rel.split("/")[0] if "/" in rel else "(root)"
            tb = by_top.setdefault(top, [0, 0])
            tb[0] += size
            tb[1] += 1
            largest.append((size, rel))

    largest.sort(reverse=True)
    return {
        "predicted_upload": {
            "bytes": upload_bytes,
            "gib": round(upload_bytes / 1073741824, 3),
            "files": upload_files,
            "by_ext": {
                k: {"bytes": v[0], "files": v[1]}
                for k, v in sorted(by_ext.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1][0])
            },
            "by_top_dir": {k: {"bytes": v[0], "files": v[1]} for k, v in sorted(by_top.items())},
        },
        "after_pruning_all_classes": {
            "bytes": sum(v[0] for v in by_class.values()),
            "files": sum(v[1] for v in by_class.values()),
            "by_class": {k: {"bytes": v[0], "files": v[1]} for k, v in sorted(by_class.items())},
        },
        "largest_included_files": [{"bytes": b, "path": p} for b, p in largest[:20]],
        "symlinks_escaping_vault": links.escaping,
        "symlinks_skipped_overlapping": links.skipped,
        "symlinks_scheduling_dependent": links.ambiguous,
        "files_over_per_file_max": too_large,
        "per_file_max_bytes": per_file_max,
        "traversal_errors": traversal_errors,
    }


def _sync_config_dir() -> Path:
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or str(Path.home() / ".config")
    return Path(base) / "obsidian-headless" / "sync"


def load_live_config(vault: Path) -> dict:
    """Find the ob sync config whose vaultPath is this vault (mirrors cli.js ``He``)."""
    root = _sync_config_dir()
    if not root.is_dir():
        raise FileNotFoundError(
            f"no ob sync state directory at {root}. Next: confirm XDG_CONFIG_HOME "
            "points at the host that runs ob, and run 'ob sync-setup' if this vault "
            "has never been connected."
        )
    unreadable: list[str] = []
    try:
        candidates = sorted(root.iterdir())
    except OSError as exc:
        raise ValueError(
            f"cannot read the ob sync state directory {root}: {exc.strerror}. "
            "Next: fix the permissions or run as the user ob runs as; pass "
            "--excluded-folders explicitly to preflight a list meanwhile."
        ) from exc
    for entry in candidates:
        config = entry / "config.json"
        try:
            if not config.is_file():
                continue
        except OSError as exc:
            unreadable.append(f"{config} ({exc.strerror})")
            continue
        try:
            data = json.loads(config.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            unreadable.append(str(config))
            continue
        # Valid JSON is not necessarily an object. A corrupt config belonging to
        # some OTHER vault must not break this vault's audit, so note and skip.
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            unreadable.append(f"{config} (JSON is {type(data).__name__}, not an object)")
            continue
        if Path(str(data.get("vaultPath", ""))).resolve() == vault:
            missing = [k for k in PERSISTED_REQUIRED_KEYS if k not in data]
            if missing:
                raise ValueError(
                    f"{config} does not look like an ob sync config "
                    f"(missing {', '.join(missing)}) — refusing to read an exclusion "
                    "list out of an unrecognized schema. Next: check the "
                    "obsidian-headless version, compare the file against the keys "
                    f"ob persists ({', '.join(PERSISTED_REQUIRED_KEYS)}), and pass "
                    "--excluded-folders explicitly to preflight a list meanwhile."
                )
            _validate_config_types(config, data)
            return data
    detail = ""
    if unreadable:
        detail = f" Unreadable config(s) skipped: {'; '.join(unreadable)}."
    raise FileNotFoundError(
        f"no ob sync configuration found for {vault}.{detail} Next: verify the vault "
        f"path matches the 'vaultPath' in a config under {root}, and run "
        "'ob sync-setup' if this vault is not connected yet."
    )


def resolve_config_dir(value: object) -> str:
    """Mirror cli.js ``ws(s)``/``Ss(s)``: a config dir must be a dotfolder NAME.

    ``Ss(s) = s && s.startsWith(".") && !s.includes("/") && !s.includes("\\")``, and
    ``ws`` THROWS ``Invalid config directory`` otherwise; a falsy value means the
    default. Accepting anything else here would scan a directory the client never
    looks at and report a confident total for it.
    """
    if not value:
        return ".obsidian"
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        raise ValueError(
            f"configDir is {value!r}, but ob stores a dotfolder name. Next: re-apply "
            "it with 'ob sync-config --config-dir .obsidian'."
        )
    if not (value.startswith(".") and "/" not in value and "\\" not in value):
        raise ValueError(
            f'Invalid config directory: "{value}". ob requires a dotfolder name '
            "(e.g. .obsidian) with no path separators, and refuses to start "
            "otherwise — so this config could not be in use as written. Next: fix it "
            "with 'ob sync-config --config-dir .obsidian'."
        )
    return value


def _validate_config_types(config: Path, data: dict) -> None:
    """Refuse a config whose fields are the wrong SHAPE or name unknown values.

    Without this a ``null``, a string or a nested list where a list-of-strings
    belongs escapes as a TypeError traceback and exit 1 — an undocumented status
    with no next action, which is the failure mode this tool exists to prevent.
    A bare string is worse than a crash: ``frozenset("app")`` is ``{'a','p'}``,
    which matches nothing and reports a confident zero.

    Driven by PERSISTED_LIST_FIELDS rather than an inline list of keys, so a field
    added to the reader cannot bypass validation. A test asserts the table covers
    every persisted key the tool reads.
    """
    for key, allowed in PERSISTED_LIST_FIELDS.items():
        if key not in data:
            continue
        value = data[key]
        if not isinstance(value, list) or not all(isinstance(v, str) for v in value):
            raise ValueError(
                f"{config}: '{key}' is {value!r}, but ob persists a list of strings "
                "there. Refusing to guess. Next: inspect the file, re-apply the "
                "setting with 'ob sync-config', or pass --excluded-folders and "
                "--file-types explicitly to preflight a list meanwhile."
            )
        if allowed is None:
            continue
        unknown = [v for v in value if v not in allowed]
        if unknown:
            raise ValueError(
                f"{config}: '{key}' names unknown value(s) {', '.join(unknown)}; ob "
                f"recognizes {', '.join(allowed)}. An unrecognized value silently "
                "matches nothing, so refusing rather than under-predicting. Next: "
                "re-apply the setting with 'ob sync-config' using a recognized value."
            )


def _resolve_file_types(args: argparse.Namespace, live: dict) -> tuple[str, ...] | None:
    """Resolve the attachment selection, honouring ob's three distinct cases."""
    if args.file_types is not None:
        if args.file_types == "":
            # cli.js: `s.fileTypes===""? delete t.allowTypes : ...` — the EXACT empty
            # string restores the defaults. " " is not it: that goes to Kr and throws.
            return DEFAULT_FILE_TYPES
        # Mirror cli.js `Kr`: split, JS-trim, lowercase, and reject ANY field that is
        # not a known type — including the empty field that "," or "image," produces.
        # Dropping empties instead would certify a selection ob refuses to apply.
        fields = tuple(_js_trim(t).lower() for t in args.file_types.split(","))
        invalid = [t for t in fields if t not in VALID_FILE_TYPES]
        if invalid:
            raise ValueError(
                f'Invalid file type: "{invalid[0]}". Valid values: '
                f"{', '.join(VALID_FILE_TYPES)}. ob rejects empty comma-separated "
                "fields too, so ',' and 'image,' are invalid. Next: pass a "
                "comma-separated list with no empty fields, or the empty string to "
                "restore ob's defaults."
            )
        return fields
    if PERSISTED_FILE_TYPES_KEY in live:
        # An empty persisted array is honoured as "no attachments": JS `[] || me`
        # keeps `[]`. Absence is the default set. These are NOT the same state.
        return tuple(live[PERSISTED_FILE_TYPES_KEY])
    return DEFAULT_FILE_TYPES


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = _Parser(
        prog="hapax-obsidian-sync-preflight",
        description="Validate an Obsidian Sync exclusion list and predict the upload set.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("vault", help="local vault path")
    source = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    source.add_argument(
        "--excluded-folders",
        help="comma-separated list to validate before applying it",
    )
    source.add_argument(
        "--from-sync-config",
        action="store_true",
        help="audit the list ob already holds for this vault",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--file-types",
        default=None,
        help=f"comma-separated subset of {','.join(VALID_FILE_TYPES)} "
        f"(empty string restores ob's defaults: {','.join(DEFAULT_FILE_TYPES)})",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--allow-missing",
        action="store_true",
        help="report unmatchable entries but exit 0 anyway",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--per-file-max",
        type=int,
        default=DEFAULT_PER_FILE_MAX,
        help="bytes above which ob skips a file (default %(default)s, the client's "
        "fallback; the SERVER may send a different perFileMax at login, which is not "
        "observable locally — pass it here if you know it)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="machine-readable output")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    vault = Path(args.vault).expanduser()
    if not vault.is_dir():
        print(
            f"not a directory: {vault}. Next: pass the local vault root "
            "(the folder ob syncs), not a note or a remote vault name.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return ERROR
    vault = vault.resolve()

    live: dict = {}
    if args.from_sync_config:
        try:
            live = load_live_config(vault)
        except (FileNotFoundError, ValueError) as exc:
            print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
            return ERROR
        # Persisted key, not the printed one — see PERSISTED_EXCLUSIONS_KEY.
        entries = list(live.get(PERSISTED_EXCLUSIONS_KEY) or [])
    else:
        entries = args.excluded_folders.split(",")

    try:
        file_types = _resolve_file_types(args, live)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        return ERROR

    configs = frozenset(live.get(PERSISTED_CONFIGS_KEY) or ())
    try:
        config_dir = resolve_config_dir(live.get("configDir"))
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        return ERROR
    findings = validate_entries(
        vault,
        entries,
        trim=not args.from_sync_config,
        config_dir=config_dir,
        configs_enabled=bool(configs),
    )
    bad = [f for f in findings if f["verdict"] != "ok"]
    effective = [f["normalized"] for f in findings if f["verdict"] == "ok"]
    # One policy across both walks: ob's watcher set is global, so a target accepted
    # in the main tree must not be re-followed from the config dir either.
    links = _LinkPolicy(vault)
    prediction = predict(vault, effective, frozenset(file_types), links, args.per_file_max)

    config_uploads = predict_config_uploads(vault, config_dir, configs, effective)
    prediction["config_uploads"] = config_uploads
    prediction["traversal_errors"].extend(config_uploads["traversal_errors"])
    prediction["predicted_upload"]["bytes"] += config_uploads["bytes"]
    prediction["predicted_upload"]["files"] += config_uploads["files"]
    prediction["predicted_upload"]["gib"] = round(
        prediction["predicted_upload"]["bytes"] / 1073741824, 3
    )

    report = {
        "vault": str(vault),
        "source": "sync-config" if args.from_sync_config else "argument",
        "vault_name": live.get("vaultName"),
        "file_types": list(file_types),
        "entries_total": len(findings),
        "entries_effective": len(effective),
        "entries_unmatchable": len(bad),
        "configs": sorted(configs),
        "findings": findings,
        **prediction,
    }

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(report, indent=2))
    else:
        print(f"vault: {vault}" + (f"  ({live['vaultName']})" if live.get("vaultName") else ""))
        print(f"file types: {','.join(file_types) or '(none — attachments will not sync)'}")
        print(f"exclusion entries: {len(findings)} total, {len(effective)} effective")
        for finding in findings:
            mark = "ok  " if finding["verdict"] == "ok" else "FAIL"
            line = f"  [{mark}] {finding['normalized']}"
            if finding["verdict"] != "ok":
                line += f"  <- {finding['detail']}"
            elif finding.get("redundant_under"):
                line += f"  (redundant under {', '.join(finding['redundant_under'])})"
            print(line)
        upload = prediction["predicted_upload"]
        print(
            f"predicted upload: {upload['bytes']} B = {upload['gib']} GiB / {upload['files']} files"
        )
        for ext, stats in list(upload["by_ext"].items())[:8]:
            print(f"  .{ext or '(none)'}: {stats['bytes']} B / {stats['files']} files")
        if prediction["files_over_per_file_max"]:
            skipped = prediction["files_over_per_file_max"]
            print(
                f"  {len(skipped)} file(s) exceed per-file max "
                f"{prediction['per_file_max_bytes']} B and are NOT counted "
                f"(largest: {max(s['bytes'] for s in skipped)} B)"
            )
        if config_uploads["files"]:
            print(
                f"  config dir {config_uploads['config_dir']}/: "
                f"{config_uploads['bytes']} B / {config_uploads['files']} files "
                f"({', '.join(sorted(configs))}) — included above"
            )
        for link in prediction["symlinks_escaping_vault"]:
            print(
                f"  NOTE: {link['path']} is a symlink OUT of the vault -> "
                f"{link['target']}; ob follows it, so everything admitted beneath "
                "it uploads. Counted above."
            )

    if prediction["symlinks_scheduling_dependent"]:
        first = prediction["symlinks_scheduling_dependent"][0]
        print(
            f"REFUSED: {len(prediction['symlinks_scheduling_dependent'])} symlink(s) "
            f"(e.g. {first['path']} -> {first['target']}) resolve to a target another "
            "link already claimed. ob scans siblings CONCURRENTLY (listRecursive awaits "
            "Promise.all), so whether one alias uploads or both do depends on which scan "
            "installs its watcher first — there is no exact prediction to give. Next: "
            "remove the duplicate alias, or exclude all but one, then re-run.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return ERROR

    if prediction["traversal_errors"]:
        first = prediction["traversal_errors"][0]
        print(
            f"REFUSED: {len(prediction['traversal_errors'])} path(s) could not be "
            f"read (e.g. {first['path']}: {first['error']}), so every total above is "
            "a FLOOR, not a measurement, and must not be used as verification "
            "evidence. Next: fix the permissions or run as the user ob runs as.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return ERROR

    if args.from_sync_config and not findings and not args.allow_missing:
        # An audit that finds no list must not read as a clean bill of health: the
        # key is absent precisely when exclusions were cleared or never set, and
        # every admitted file then uploads.
        print(
            f"REFUSED: the live sync config for {vault} configures NO exclusions "
            f"('{PERSISTED_EXCLUSIONS_KEY}' absent or empty), so nothing is excluded "
            f"and all {prediction['predicted_upload']['files']} admitted files would "
            "upload. Next: set them with 'ob sync-config --excluded-folders', or pass "
            "--allow-missing if an empty exclusion list is intended.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return REFUSED

    if bad and not args.allow_missing:
        print(
            f"REFUSED: {len(bad)} exclusion entr{'y' if len(bad) == 1 else 'ies'} "
            "cannot match anything. ob accepts them silently and the data they name "
            "WOULD UPLOAD. Next: fix the paths shown above, or pass --allow-missing "
            "if that is intended.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return REFUSED
    return OK


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
